UR  - https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S095058492600217X
KW  - Source code
KW  - Vulnerability detection
KW  - Code aware transformer-based methods
KW  - Deep neural network models
KW  - Imbalance aware
AB  - Context:
The deploying automated vulnerability detectors for source code in practical settings is hindered by key challenges including severe class imbalance, domain shift, and limited generalization across real-world benchmarks.
Objective:
This study proposes a lightweight and scalable approach to improve vulnerability detection performance, particularly in imbalanced and resource-constrained environments, while ensuring generalizability across diverse codebases.
Methods:
This study introduces VulnScout, a model that integrates frozen transformer-based code embeddings with an enhanced multilayer perceptron (MLP) classifier trained using adaptive focal loss. To handle long input sequences that exceed token limits, VulnScout employs overlapping chunking and mean pooling of segment-level embeddings. A hybrid imbalance-handling strategy, combining data-level sampling and loss-level reweighting, is employed to better capture rare vulnerability patterns. The model is evaluated through nearly one hundred experiments across six public datasets encompassing diverse projects and programming domains.
Results:
VulnScout consistently achieves higher recall and F1 scores than fine-tuned transformer and graph-based baselines. It also demonstrates improved robustness under stringent false-positive constraints, as measured by VD-S (FNR@FPR≤0.5%) and Matthews Correlation Coefficient (MCC). Unlike resource-intensive fine-tuning approaches, VulnScout runs efficiently on mid-range GPUs, such as the RTX 3060 with 12GB VRAM, enabling broader accessibility and lower deployment costs.
Conclusion:
VulnScout provides a practical and generalizable solution for detecting source code vulnerabilities. By addressing class imbalance, sequence length limitations, and hardware constraints, it serves as an effective alternative to fine-tuned models, facilitating real-world adoption of vulnerability detection systems.
