AB  - The quick development of OpenAI’s Large Language Models (LLM) has contributed extensively to detecting vulnerabilities in software code, hence transforming the traditional pattern-matching procedures into powerful, semantic-based detection of code vulnerabilities. In this study, we introduce a comprehensive analysis and performance comparison of OpenAI evolution for the twelve models introduced during the period from the initial GPT model introduced in 2018 up to the current GPT-5 introduced in 2025. The research introduced three datasets used as a benchmark for code vulnerability detection, namely, DiversVul, BigVul, and CVEFixes. The datasets contain 39,700 vulnerable functions with 155 distinct code weaknesses spreading over 1,281 open-source projects. The primary results of this research showed a high performance progress. For example, the F1 score is increased from 28.3% in the starting GPT to 62.4% in the last model (GPT-5). In addition, this study showed there is a weakness in the current models called ”generalization gap” as the detection performance is decreased by 65-80% when exposed to unseen software projects. The analysis also showed different scaling patterns with different vulnerability categories. In the injection vulnerability, the improvement is shown to be near-linear, while it showed diminishing returns with the memory safety issues. The research provides crucial insights into the architectural determinants of performance, the relationship between model scale and detection capability, and the practical implications for real-world security tool deployment. These findings establish essential benchmarks for the research community and illuminate promising directions for future work in AI-driven software security analysis.
