AB  - Smart contract vulnerabilities have resulted in financial losses exceeding $3.8 billion since 2016, yet existing detection methods remain inadequate. Static analysis tools produce false positive rates above 40% while missing entire vulnerability classes. Sequential deep learning models overlook structural dependencies inherent in program semantics. Graph neural networks operate at single resolution levels, and expert pattern approaches demand manual engineering with limited generalization. However, no current method captures multi-scale vulnerability patterns that span local code constructs and global contract ecosystems a gap that constrains detection of structurally complex exploits such as cross-function reentrancy. This paper presents a hierarchical graph transformer with adaptive community integration that addresses this multi-resolution limitation. The architecture employs six progressive refinement stages for feature abstraction, learned gating mechanisms that dynamically weight community context across three hierarchy levels, and uncertainty-based multi-task learning that jointly optimizes contract classification and function-level vulnerability localization. Evaluation on 9742 Ethereum smart contracts yields 92.14% accuracy, 92.18% F1-score, and 0.967 AUROC, surpassing the strongest baseline by 1.99 percentage points in F1-score (Cohen’s d = 0.812, p = 0.010). Five-fold cross-validation with Bonferroni-corrected ablation analysis confirms that each core component contributes significantly. Fine-grained evaluation across four reentrancy sub-types, alongside comparisons with recent large language model-augmented and graph-based detectors, further validates robustness. The system achieves 20.8 millisecond inference latency, enabling deployment within continuous security auditing workflows. Source code and experimental artifacts are publicly available at https://anonymous.4open.science/r/HGT-ACI-E9C5/.
