AB  - Intelligent vulnerability detection techniques based on deep neural networks (DNNs) have gained increasing attention in recent years due to their end-to-end feature extraction abilities. However, existing DNN-based detection models are highly sensitive to code style, programming language, variable names, etc., which can be exploited by backdoor attacks. The difficulty of compressing redundant semantics in vulnerability information to eliminate triggers increases as the stealthiness of backdoor attacks improves. To address this problem, we propose, a novel redundant semantic LLM compression (ReSLC) framework to defend against backdoor attacks in DNN-based intelligent vulnerability detection models. It uses a prompt-based and expert-free strategy to normalize code semantics and reduce spurious correlations, thus improving the DNN model’s robustness. It is based on the core hypothesis that the robustness of the model depends on the purity of the semantic space. We design a two-stage preprocessing pipeline: 1) Trigger-clean module, which removes misleading elements such as unused variables, functions, and comments; 2) Feature-extend module, which adds meaningful semantic cues to better highlight vulnerability-relevant patterns. Both stages are powered by large language models (LLMs) through prompt-based code manipulation, eliminating the need for handcrafted rules or expert knowledge. Experiments show that ReSLC reduces the attack success rate by up to 73.77%, while consistently improving model performance, with the accuracy gains exceeding 2% and notable improvements in other key metrics. These results show that the framework can effectively defend against attacks and improve the performance of vulnerability detection models in different settings.
