AB  - The increasing use of Large Language Models (LLMs) in cybersecurity creates opportunities to automate mappings between vulnerability descriptions and adversarial behavior frameworks such as MITRE ATT&CK. However, LLM-generated CVE/CWE→ATT&CK mappings remain difficult to assess because plausible associations may lack explicit evidence and traceability. This work frames mapping verification as an intelligent information-processing problem and proposes a post-hoc Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) framework that evaluates LLM-generated mappings through two complementary dimensions: textual similarity and Evidence Support. The framework decomposes each generated mapping into four claims — type, vector, impact, and target — and evaluates each claim against external cybersecurity knowledge retrieved from CWE, CAPEC, and MITRE ATT&CK. The verifier uses the retrieved evidence and an explicit similarity-based decision criterion to produce a traceable claim–evidence–score–verdict chain with operational supported or not_supported verdicts. The framework was evaluated on 100 CVE–ATT&CK evaluation cases involving 93 distinct CVE identifiers, producing 400 claims and covering 61 distinct ATT&CK techniques. Stage 1 showed that textual-similarity metrics only partially align with expert judgment; at the holistic level, MPNet cosine similarity showed the strongest association with aggregated expert ratings (ρ=0.4909), while attribute-level associations varied substantially. In Stage 2, 145 of 400 claims (36.25%) received a supported verdict under the operational threshold τ=0.65. Calibration against the concordant human reference characterized this threshold as a conservative operating point, with precision of 0.890 and specificity of 0.862 for supported verdicts, at the cost of a recall of 0.482. SupportRate also varied across target (60%), vector (35%), impact (30%), and type (20%). These results show that textual similarity and Evidence Support provide complementary but non-equivalent signals. The proposed framework therefore adds claim-level evidence traceability to LLM-generated cybersecurity mappings while preserving human oversight for cases that require further review.
