AB  - With the explosive growth of network traffic, the scale and complexity of attacks carried by traffic are constantly increasing. Existing traffic classification methods primarily focus on binary classification or coarse-grained multi-classification, while lacking the ability to accurately identify the specific exploited vulnerability. To bridge this gap, we propose a three-stage framework that combines retrieval technology with large language model (LLM) reasoning to attribute attack traffic to specific vulnerabilities. Specifically, we construct a high-quality database which contains CVE-labeled threat intelligence and supports dual-channel indexing. Next, we introduce a hybrid retrieval strategy to generate candidate threat intelligence set for each attack traffic. Finally, we design structured prompts to guide LLM to re-rank the candidate threat intelligence and output the most relevant CVE ID. Experiments on large-scale real traffic data show the CVE attribution accuracy can reach 91.50%. In real-world deployment, our method can identify 1100 to 1300 CVE vulnerability types per week, and effectively processes 657 to 794 million attack requests. These results confirm the effectiveness of our method for fine-grained traffic classification.
