AB  - Security concerns continue to grow, particularly in the IoT domain. Therefore, systems have been developed to assess and track vulnerabilities and their severity. A widely adopted standard for this task is the Common Vulnerability Scoring System (CVSS), which is currently in its fourth version. However, the conversion from version 3.x to version 4 is challenging. Manual conversion is time-consuming and error-prone. This work proposes an automated framework to promote the adoption of CVSS v4.0. It first converts structured CVSS v3.x vectors into CVSS v4.0 through a machine-learning-assisted pipeline. The framework is extended with a fine-tuned LLM capable of inferring the required CVSS v3.x vector directly from textual vulnerability descriptions, enabling end-to-end scoring. The pipeline is evaluated against CVEs holding official scores under both CVSS v3.x and v4.0 in the National Vulnerability Database, kept strictly disjoint from all training and label-completion stages. On these CVEs, it achieves an adjusted conversion accuracy of 73.9%, underestimating severity in 7.1% of cases. The LLM component is assessed separately, under a domain-transfer setting. When fine-tuned on general-domain CVEs and evaluated on a held-out IoT-specific set, it achieves 80.4% CVSS metric prediction accuracy, with a standard deviation of about 1.336 points in the difference between predicted and reference scores. In the general-domain evaluation, the best model (Mistral v0.3) achieves the lowest average deviation of 1.058 points. As the two components are assessed on different populations, their figures are complementary rather than directly comparable. These findings show the effectiveness of the proposed solution for automating CVSS version migration.
