AB  - Industrial control systems (ICS) require vulnerability prioritization that reflects adversarial behavior and operational consequences, particularly when patching is constrained by safety and availability requirements. However, existing vulnerability-to-ATT&CK approaches are largely designed for general IT environments and often rely on direct CVE-to-technique mapping, providing limited support for ICS-specific context, multi-label inference, and downstream operational impacts. This study proposes VulnTACT, a lightweight framework for predicting MITRE ATT&CK for ICS tactics and techniques from heterogeneous vulnerability evidence. Rather than directly mapping vulnerability descriptions to ATT&CK labels, VulnTACT performs five ICS-aware reasoning steps covering vulnerability type, enabled functionality, exploitation technique, affected object, and adversarial tactic. The intermediate results and retrieved evidence are synthesized into category-specific candidates for exploitation techniques, primary impacts, and secondary impacts, followed by a ranked multi-label prediction. The reasoning structure is informed by MITRE’s CVE Mapping Methodology, while retrieval and QLoRA-based domain adaptation provide vulnerability-specific evidence and alignment with recurring ICS terminology and cross-source security semantics. Experiments using a 2024 validation set, a temporally separated 2025 test set, and 59 CTI-derived cases show improvements of approximately 7–12 percentage points over the strongest baseline across ranking metrics. Ablation results confirm the complementary contributions of retrieval, domain adaptation, and structured five-step reasoning. VulnTACT therefore supports resource-efficient and traceable vulnerability-to-ATT&CK mapping for threat-informed prioritization and compensating-control selection in patch-constrained ICS environments.
