KW  - Software engineering
AB  - Large Language Models (LLMs) have shown strong potential in detecting vulnerabilities across diverse codebases. However, their effectiveness heavily depends on prompt design. Most existing prompting strategies are tailored for natural language tasks and fail to capture the semantic and structural characteristics required for program analysis. As a result, their performance degrades in specialized domains such as smart contract vulnerability detection. Addressing this gap, we propose SAGE-Prompt, a new Semantic-Aware Graph-Enhanced prompting technique to improve the performance of LLMs in Solidity vulnerability detection. SAGE-Prompt introduces two key mechanisms: semantic-enriched context specification, which helps LLMs interpret execution semantics encoded in graphs, and semantic example retrieval, which enriches prompts with similar vulnerable contract patterns. Together, these mechanisms guide LLMs on both “what to analyze” and “how to inference” about potential exploits. It improves LLM-based smart contract vulnerability detection by exposing execution relevant semantic informations, such as Control Flow Graphs, Call Graphs, and Data Dependency, along with similar vulnerable code retrieved through an example retrieval mechanism. By organizing these information in a structured and vulnerability aware manner, the prompt guides the model’s analysis toward execution semantics and exploit-relevant code regions, without modifying the LLM architecture. We evaluate SAGE-Prompt against generic prompting (i.e., zero-shot, few-shot, and chain-of-thought) and state-of-the-art Solidity vulnerability-oriented prompting approaches (i.e., GPTLens, LLM4Vuln, and VulnHunt-GPT) across four representative LLMs and three benchmark datasets. SAGE-Prompt consistently surpasses all baselines in both binary and multi-class classification, achieving accuracy improvements of up to 10.83% on SBCurated, 11.19% on CodeSmell, and 12.65% on SolidiFI over the few-shot baseline. Finally, ablation studies further confirm that SAGE-Prompt’s configuration, combining semantic enrichment with guided in-context learning, is the most effective design among all evaluated variants. This establishes SAGE-Prompt as a lightweight, generalizable, and high-performing alternative for smart contract vulnerability detection.
