AB  - Server-Side Request Forgery (SSRF) allows attackers to exploit a target server for accessing internal services or restricted resources, thereby bypassing access controls and posing significant risks to web applications. Existing SSRF detection approaches predominantly rely on rigid rules or manual feature engineering, thereby struggling with complex control flows, cross-function dependencies, and the scarcity of high-quality labeled data, which often leads to high false-negative and false-positive rates. To overcome these limitations, we propose SSRFinder, a context-aware SSRF detection and validation framework that integrates static taint analysis, pre-trained language models, and dynamic verification. SSRFinder models external input sources, SSRF-related sink functions, and filtering operations as a unified attack surface. Based on this abstraction, it defines an SSRF risk function to prioritize high-risk code regions and guide subsequent learning-based analysis. It then leverages Program Dependency Graphs (PDGs) to capture syntactic, control-flow, and data-flow dependencies, enabling the extraction of precise taint slices while suppressing irrelevant noise. To enhance semantic understanding, SSRFinder incorporates the RoBERTa pre-trained model, which facilitates more accurate modeling of contextual and logical dependencies within code slices. Finally, a payload-mutation-based dynamic validation mechanism generates diverse URL variants and injects them into controlled execution environments to confirm the actual exploitability of predicted SSRF slices, thereby mitigating false positives. We implemented a prototype of SSRFinder and evaluated it on 6045 code slices extracted from 21 real-world web applications. SSRFinder successfully identified 70 SSRF vulnerabilities and uncovered 5 previously unknown issues that were missed by four state-of-the-art static analysis tools, demonstrating its effectiveness and robustness in real-world SSRF detection scenarios.
