KW  - Software vulnerabilities
KW  - Large language models
KW  - Vulnerability datasets
KW  - Software security
KW  - AI security
AB  - Software vulnerabilities remain a major threat to modern software systems, and recent advances in Machine Learning (ML), Deep Learning (DL), and Large Language Models (LLMs) have intensified interest in automated vulnerability detection. At the core of these techniques are vulnerability datasets, which provide the training and evaluation foundations for developing and benchmarking detection models. This survey presents a structured review of existing vulnerability datasets, covering synthetic tests, real-world benchmarks, and large-scale collections mined from open-source ecosystems. We analyze their construction pipelines, representational choices, and the trade-offs between synthetic versus real-world data; function- versus file- and project-level granularity; and single- versus multi-project coverage in the context of ML-, DL-, and LLM-based vulnerability analysis. Our analysis reveals recurring challenges, including limited availability of large-scale, high-quality real-world data; severe class imbalance and vulnerability-type skew; noisy and inconsistent labels; restricted coverage of certain vulnerability families; duplication and redundancy; weak generalization to industrial codebases; and pervasive reproducibility gaps. Building on these observations, we outline future directions for dataset design, emphasizing larger and more diverse benchmarks, multi-language and multi-modal integration, patch-aware and evolution-aware constructions, fine-grained and explainability-oriented annotations, continual maintenance, and standardized evaluation protocols. Finally, we highlight the emerging role of LLMs not only as detection models but also as instruments for dataset construction, supporting semi-automated vulnerability mining, annotation, deduplication, and targeted synthetic data generation within hybrid workflows that combine static analysis, LLM-based reasoning, and human oversight. By consolidating existing resources and articulating open issues, this survey provides a roadmap for the next generation of robust, scalable, and trustworthy vulnerability datasets to support research and practice in software security.
