AB  - Cyber ranges are essential training platforms for security teams; however, creating intentionally vulnerable environments remains labor-intensive. While modern virtualization technologies like Docker enable “Infrastructure as Code” approaches, and large language models (LLMs) significantly reduce the effort required for code generation, creating exploitable scenarios remains a challenge. It requires resolving dependencies, configuring interconnected services, and ensuring explicit exploit preconditions, yet the effectiveness of LLMs in realizing these scenarios remains largely unexplored. This paper presents a systematic exploratory study of LLM-assisted generation of intentionally vulnerable containerized environments. We compare different generation paradigms (service-based vs. web applications, package-based vs. source-based builds, and single-container vs. compose-based setups) under zero-shot conditions. We evaluate six LLMs using a two-phase protocol: first, we select the model with the lowest mean Weighted Error Score (WES); second, we conduct an in-depth analysis of paired deployment strategies across 22 vulnerabilities (12 services and 10 web applications), totaling 44 generation attempts and 40 analyzed scenarios (following four ethical refusals). Key findings establish a clear feasibility boundary. While state-of-the-art models (e.g., Gemini3-Pro, GPT-5.2) reduced build failure rates from 81.5% to 51.9%, their ability to generate fully functional, exploitable environments remains limited. Specifically, while functional correctness (build/run) reaches 37.5%–50.0% depending on the strategy, the transition to exploitability represents the primary failure point, with only 5.0% (2/40) of analyzed scenarios proving exploitable. We introduce a repair-oriented analysis based on two metrics to evaluate the change impact and automation challenges of fixing incorrect Dockerfiles. This analysis demonstrates that the choice of strategy significantly affects the repair burden: package-based services require higher structural and automation effort than source-based counterparts, while compose-based web deployments yield better functional outcomes than bundled configurations. Furthermore, our results highlight a practical trade-off in service deployment: despite higher build complexity, source-based reconstruction is preferable for vulnerability fidelity. This is because distribution packages often include downstream security backports that silently mitigate vulnerabilities, even when version identifiers suggest susceptibility. Finally, we provide a taxonomy of build, execution, and exploitation errors. All experimental data, including AI-generated Dockerfiles with annotations and prompts, are publicly available. This study establishes both the potential and the current limitations of zero-shot LLM-based generation, serving as a benchmark for future research into tool-augmented approaches.
