KW  - Label Noise
AB  - Recent work has shown that reinforcement learning with Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO) can outperform Supervised Fine-Tuning (SFT) for training small language models on vulnerability detection. However, prior studies rely on benchmarks with noisy, automatically generated labels, leaving open how label quality interacts with the choice of training paradigm. We present a controlled 2x2x2 factorial study comparing training method (SFT vs. GRPO) x label quality (human-verified PrimeVul vs. noisy Big-Vul) x data scale (1k vs. 5k samples) on Llama-3.2-1B-Instruct. Two findings emerge: (i) GRPO consistently outperforms SFT, and its advantage widens as labels become noisier; and (ii) 1,000 cleanly labeled samples outperform 5,000 noisily labeled ones under both paradigms. These results suggest that, for vulnerability detection under realistic data constraints, investment in label verification yields larger returns than scaling noisy data, and that GRPO should be preferred when label quality cannot be guaranteed.
